`ca::is_character<T>`: the code unit types strings are made of, and the encoding each one
carries throughout ca.

| Type | Encoding |
|---|---|
| `char` | UTF-8 by convention |
| `char8_t` | UTF-8 |
| `char16_t` | UTF-16 |
| `char32_t` | UTF-32: one unit per code point |
| `wchar_t` | UTF-32 where it has 4 bytes (Linux, macOS), UTF-16 where it has 2 (Windows) |

The concept constrains every string type and view (`sv::basic<C>`, `basic_str<C, A>`) and the
unicode functions, which pick their decoder by the size of the unit. Sizes and indices always
count code units; code points are counted only by `ca::unicode` and the formatter's width.

```c++
#include "ca/text.hpp"

template <ca::is_character C>
[[nodiscard]] constexpr auto units_of(const C* text) noexcept -> unsigned {
	unsigned n = 0U;
	while (text[n] != C{})
		++n;
	return n;
}

static_assert(units_of(u8"é") == 2U);
static_assert(units_of(u"é") == 1U);
static_assert(units_of(U"\U0001F600") == 1U);
static_assert(units_of(u"\U0001F600") == 2U);
static_assert(!ca::is_character<signed char> && !ca::is_character<unsigned char>);
```

`signed char` and `unsigned char` are not characters: they are small integers, and
`unsigned char` is `ca::byte`, raw memory.
